= i
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Three suitable examples are **<cubes> bounded mainly by $\{100\}$ <facets>, <octahedra> bounded by $\{111\}$ <facets>, and <truncated octahedra> bounded by both $\{111\}$ and $\{100\}$ <facets>**. These are crystallographically distinct shapes rather than three arbitrary rounded particle outlines.

At fixed volume the <thermodynamic equilibrium> shape minimizes $G_{\rm surf}=\sum_i\gamma_iA_i$, with an edge/corner correction when particles are very small. In the <Wulff construction>, <facet> support-plane distances scale as their interfacial free energies. <Silver>'s clean low-index energies, selective <adsorption> of <ligands> or ions, <solvent> <chemical potential> and possible support contact alter those energies. <Crystal twinning> and elastic <strain> can add further costs or stabilize multiply twinned forms. A face with slow attachment kinetics can also survive during growth even when that morphology is not the <thermodynamic equilibrium> Wulff shape. <Homogeneous nucleation> describes where nuclei form; it does not guarantee an <thermodynamic equilibrium> final shape.

= ii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In a <polyol process>, a hot polyol such as <ethylene glycol> acts as <solvent> and reductant for a <silver> precursor. <Nucleation> creates seeds; controlled <reduction> supplies atoms for their subsequent growth, while <polyvinylpyrrolidone> <adsorption> and seed <crystal twinning> favour anisotropic growth. For the common <silver nanowire> route, a fivefold-twinned seed can develop into a pentagonal wire with predominantly $\{100\}$ side faces and growth at less-passivated ends. Cross-sectional microscopy and <diffraction> of microtomed wires provide evidence for that twinned structure rather than an inference from a long outline alone.<Polyol Synthesis of Uniform Silver Nanowires: A Plausible Growth Mechanism and the Supporting Evidence by Yugang Sun and others>[Nano Letters 3 (2003), pp. 955–960, pentagonal cross sections and fivefold twinning]{ref}

The <aspect ratio> depends on seed population and twin survival, relative end/side growth rates, precursor feed and <reduction> rate, <PVP> coverage, <temperature>, trace halides, dissolved oxygen and growth time. High <aspect ratio> requires a suitable population of twinned seeds, suppression of competing particle <nucleation> and continued atom supply while sidewall thickening is restrained. These variables must be controlled together; adding more capping <polymer> alone is not a universal recipe. Advantages are solution processing, substantial yield and tunable dimensions without an etched hard template. Disadvantages include sensitive batch reproducibility, particle by-products, residual insulating <ligand>, purification, oxidation/tarnish and <nanowire junction resistance> in later films. Compare seed/capping/halide controls using microscopy and size distributions, not only solution colour.

= iii
{parent=19}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A transparent metallic network must span the patterned electrode with many redundant current paths while obscuring little area. For random rods, number density times squared wire length is a useful <percolation> measure; greater <aspect ratio> generally reaches a connected network at lower coverage. Conductivity also depends on intrinsic metal resistivity, wire cross section, orientation, length distribution, wire–wire <contact resistance> and the number of junctions along each spanning path. Insulating capping layers can make <nanowire junction resistance> dominate even when the individual wires are excellent conductors.

Near the connectivity threshold, small density changes or wire breaks produce large resistance changes. For a narrow stripe, finite-size effects and orientation matter: a macroscopic sheet-average <percolation> result does not ensure a reliable 100 µm-wide path. Optical performance depends on coverage, metal extinction, wire diameter, aggregation, <optical haze>, substrate transmission and any binder. Increasing wire density lowers resistance but usually lowers transmission. <Welding>, pressing or suitable embedding can improve contacts with less additional optical coverage than simply adding metal.<Self-limited plasmonic welding of silver nanowire junctions by Erik C. Garnett and others>[Nature Materials 11 (2012), pp. 241–249, junction-localized welding mechanism]{ref}

The stripe has $L/W=250$ squares, so its end-to-end resistance is $R=250R_\square$; **the stated sheet-resistance limit requires a stripe resistance below 5 kΩ**, not 20 Ω. The sketch below separates sparse connectivity, redundant paths and junction quality.

\Image[paper-2-transparent-networks.png]
{title=Original network schematics distinguishing connectivity, optical coverage and wire-junction resistance}

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Three credible architectures are **a welded <silver nanowire> network; a thin <silver nanowire> network with a <conducting polymer> overcoat; and a nanocrystalline <silver> grid formed from <nanoparticle ink>**. <Silver> provides low intrinsic resistivity. A sparse long-wire network can be both flexible and highly transmitting if its junctions are well connected. A thin, optically weak <conducting polymer> overcoat can bridge junctions and smooth the surface, provided its added absorption and moisture sensitivity are controlled. A fine grid offers a more directly designed optical/electrical trade-off and does not rely on operating near random <percolation>.

For a quantitative grid feasibility screen, take pitch $p=5\ \mathrm{\mu m}$, line width $w=100\ \mathrm{nm}$ and metal thickness $t=200\ \mathrm{nm}$. An opaque-line square-grid estimate gives $T_{\rm grid}\simeq(1-w/p)^2=0.9604$. With a transmitting <polymer> substrate of 0.95, the combined estimate is 0.912. The <sheet resistance> is approximately $R_\square=\rho_{\rm eff}p/(wt)$. Even an effective <silver> resistivity four times $1.6\times10^{-8}\ \Omega\,\mathrm m$ gives **$R_\square\simeq16\ \Omega$**, with a coating thinner than 400 nm. There are about 20 mesh periods across the stripe, making the finite-width design more credible than a pitch comparable with its whole width.

These are feasible material/process choices, not guaranteed specifications for every film carrying those names. For the two <nanowire> architectures, choose wire dimensions, loading and junction treatment to pass the same finished-stripe measurements; the hybrid's optical budget must include its overcoat. Measure transmission at 550 nm with its reference stated, <sheet resistance>, profile thickness, bend-cycle stability and pattern continuity. Bare nanotube or <graphene> networks should not be assumed to meet this demanding resistance–transmission combination without additional metallic conduction or other demonstrated improvement.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the <silver nanowire> network, synthesize or source long, narrow wires, remove excess <ligand> without causing aggregation, formulate a stable coating ink and coat the flexible web by a scalable method such as <slot-die coating> or <gravure printing>. Dry below the substrate's damage <temperature>, then press or locally weld junctions and embed or cap the network if required. Define 100 µm stripes by masked coating, a printing-compatible patterned <wetting> layer, or post-coating <laser ablation>; provide robust end contacts and remove stray bridges between adjacent stripes. Roll processing offers throughput, but <coffee-ring effect>, wire alignment, random weak links and ablation damage require inline optical/electrical inspection. Verify that the local junction treatment improves resistance without simply darkening the whole film.

For the <silver> nanoparticle-derived grid, use a repeatable <nanoimprint lithography> to form a fine mesh inside the outer 25 mm by 100 µm stripe pattern. Fill the channels with a <silver> <nanoparticle ink>, remove excess from the transparent openings, and consolidate the metal by substrate-compatible chemical, photonic or low-temperature thermal <sintering>. Transfer or embed the grid into the <polymer> surface and add electrical pads. The quantitative design in part (b) sets target pitch, line width and thickness; the large-scale process must maintain those dimensions and a continuous metal cross section. A master enables repeated replication and deterministic open area, but tooling cost, incomplete filling, residual metal in windows, shrinkage cracks, imperfect <sintering> and adhesion are important limitations. Test the finished patterned stripes, rather than relying on a large unpatterned test coupon alone.

= 20
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= i
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <two-dimensional electron gas> is confined into discrete subbands perpendicular to an interface while retaining nearly free motion in its plane. It is not a literal zero-thickness <electron> sheet. In a <GaAs>/<AlGaAs> <semiconductor heterostructure>, the <band offset> and self-consistent <band bending> confine <electrons> on the <GaAs> side. Donors in <AlGaAs> can supply <electrons> while an undoped spacer separates them from the ionized dopants, reducing impurity scattering. This modulation-doping arrangement yields a high-mobility channel, with the lowest confinement subband occupied when the confinement spacing exceeds the Fermi and thermal scales. Gates tune the density or locally deplete the channel into constrictions. Low-temperature Hall and longitudinal measurements test the resulting sheet density and transport quality.

= ii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the magnitudes of the single-electron-band Drude Hall relation and longitudinal sheet resistivity:
$$
|\rho_{xy}|=\frac{B}{n_se},\qquad \rho_{xx}=\frac1{n_se\mu}.
$$
The given values are two-dimensional resistivities, so no sample thickness is inserted. Thus
$$
\boxed{n_s=\frac1{(1.602\times10^{-19})(2083)}
=2.997\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}=2.997\times10^{11}\ \mathrm{cm^{-2}},}
$$
$$
\boxed{\mu=\frac{|\rho_{xy}|}{B\rho_{xx}}=\frac{2083}{0.6}
=3.472\times10^3\ \mathrm{m^2\,V^{-1}s^{-1}}
=3.472\times10^7\ \mathrm{cm^2\,V^{-1}s^{-1}}.}
$$
The carrier sign follows from a specified Hall-voltage orientation; these calculations use positive magnitudes. A parallel conducting layer, several occupied subbands or a quantized Hall plateau would require a different interpretation of a single-field measurement.

= iii
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a parabolic <two-dimensional electron gas> with one valley and two spin states, the <density of states> per area is $g_{2D}=m^*/(\pi\hbar^2)$. Integrating from the subband edge to the zero-temperature <Fermi energy> gives $n_s=g_{2D}E_F$, hence
$$
\boxed{E_F=\frac{\pi\hbar^2n_s}{m^*}=1.716\times10^{-21}\ \mathrm J
=10.71\ \mathrm{meV}.}
$$
Here $m^*=0.067(9.109\times10^{-31})=6.103\times10^{-32}\ \mathrm{kg}$, and the energy is measured from the occupied subband bottom. Equivalently $k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_s}=1.3722\times10^8\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{v_F=\frac{\hbar k_F}{m^*}=\sqrt{\frac{2E_F}{m^*}}
=2.371\times10^5\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The spin factor is already included in $g_{2D}$; counting it again would halve the energy incorrectly.

= iv
{parent=20}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

This is **<transverse magnetic focusing>**. The depleted gates form reflecting boundaries and two constrictions. A narrow <quantum point contact> collimates carriers into cyclotron trajectories with <cyclotron radius> $r_c=m^*v_F/(e|B|)=\hbar k_F/(e|B|)$. When an integer number of semicircular skipping arcs links the two openings, the collector couples particularly strongly to the injector population. A floating voltage contact then changes its <electrochemical potential> to satisfy zero net current, producing a peak in the measured nonlocal voltage. Sweeping the field changes the arc diameter, moving the focused population into or away from the collector.

The connection condition is $L=2jr_c$ for integer $j\ge1$, with the higher orders requiring specular reflections. The effect needs <ballistic electron transport>, sufficiently narrow openings and a smooth boundary. <Electron phase coherence> can add interference structure, but the basic geometrical focusing condition is semiclassical.<Coherent electron focusing with quantum point contacts in a two-dimensional electron gas by H. van Houten and others>[Physical Review B 39 (1989), pp. 8556–8575, focusing geometry and peak structure]{ref}

There is a polarity qualification in the printed geometry. The <electron> charge is negative: for a field out of the page, an <electron> launched upward through the left opening bends left, not toward the right opening. A direct left-opening-to-right-opening focusing path in the upper region therefore requires the field into the page; the printed outward field supports the reverse connection. Reversing conventional current reverses the occupation perturbation and voltage sign, but does not reverse the curvature of an individual <electron>. Thus the standard injector-to-collector explanation describes the focusing mechanism, while the asserted signed voltage increase requires a consistent field/lead convention. The calculated results below are peak-field magnitudes; taking outward field positive gives negative fields for direct left-to-right electron injection.

\Image[paper-2-electron-focusing.png]
{title=Original semicircular connections for three focusing orders; trajectories are shown without carrier-direction arrows}

= b
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substituting $r_c=\hbar k_F/(e|B|)$ into $L=2jr_c$ gives
$$
B_j=\frac{2j\hbar k_F}{eL},\qquad L=10^{-6}\ \mathrm m.
$$
Using the density-derived Fermi <wave vector> gives the first three peak magnitudes
$$
\boxed{|B_1|=0.1807\ \mathrm T,\qquad |B_2|=0.3613\ \mathrm T,\qquad |B_3|=0.5420\ \mathrm T.}
$$
The equally spaced sequence follows from the number of reflected half-orbits, not from successive quantum Hall fillings. The field polarity selecting a given injector–collector orientation follows from the <Lorentz force> direction.

= c
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The transport <mean free path> is $\ell=v_F\tau_{\rm tr}$, while $\mu=e\tau_{\rm tr}/m^*$. Eliminating the scattering time gives $\ell=\mu\hbar k_F/e$. The usual <ballistic focusing mobility criterion> $\ell\gtrsim L$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mu_{\rm min}\sim\frac{eL}{\hbar k_F}=11.1\ \mathrm{m^2\,V^{-1}s^{-1}}
=1.11\times10^5\ \mathrm{cm^2\,V^{-1}s^{-1}}.}
$$
A more geometrically faithful first-peak survival criterion uses the semicircular path length $s=\pi L/2$, giving $\mu\gtrsim17.4\ \mathrm{m^2\,V^{-1}s^{-1}}$. There is no sharp universal visibility threshold: clearly resolved peaks favour $\ell\gg s$, small aperture broadening and <specular reflection>. The mobility inferred in part (ii) corresponds to $\ell\simeq314\ \mathrm{\mu m}$, comfortably above both estimates. Higher-order peaks can still be reduced by boundary roughness even when bulk mobility is high.

= v
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Treat the top gate as a <parallel-plate capacitor>, neglecting fringing and assuming its voltage change couples to the 2DEG without a dominant trap or series-capacitance correction. Its <capacitance> per area and induced <electron> density are
$$
\frac CA=\frac{\epsilon_0\epsilon_r}{d}=1.15102\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{F\,m^{-2}},\qquad
\Delta n_s=\frac{\epsilon_0\epsilon_r\Delta V}{ed}
=1.43698\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}.
$$
In this <top-gate density tuning>, a positive gate change increases the <electron> density in this model, giving $n_s'=4.43371\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}$. Since $B_j\propto k_F\propto\sqrt{n_s}$,
$$
\boxed{|B'_1|=0.2197\ \mathrm T,\qquad |B'_2|=0.4395\ \mathrm T,\qquad |B'_3|=0.6592\ \mathrm T.}
$$
The fields rise by a factor $\sqrt{n_s'/n_s}=1.2163$, not by the density ratio itself. Finite <quantum capacitance> is a small correction here: the one-subband value $e^2m^*/(\pi\hbar^2)$ is much larger than the stated geometric <capacitance> per area.

= vi
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The highest-field plateau is labelled $R_H/(h/e^2)=1$, so it is the **$\nu=1$ plateau** in the <filling factor>, with the longitudinal resistivity returning to approximately zero after the transition near **10 T**. The odd integer indicates a resolved spin branch at these fields; assuming a permanent twofold <spin degeneracy> for every orbital <Landau level> would misidentify the plotted sequence.

For a nominal exactly filled $\nu=1$ state at a chosen field $B_*$, the density target is $n_*=eB_*/h$. Combining this with the same gate capacitor model gives the required gate-voltage change relative to the original ungated-density reference:
$$
\boxed{\Delta V_g(B_*)=\frac{ed}{\epsilon_0\epsilon_r}
\left(\frac{eB_*}{h}-n_s\right).}
$$
Taking $B_*\simeq10\ \mathrm T$ as the graph's onset-scale estimate gives $n_*=2.418\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}$ and
$$
\boxed{\Delta V_g\simeq-0.0806\ \mathrm V.}
$$
Relative to the positive 0.2 V setting in part (v), this is an additional change of approximately **$-0.281\ \mathrm V$**. An absolute laboratory gate voltage cannot be obtained without its initial offset or threshold; the calculated quantities are voltage changes.

There is a graphical/model qualification: a <quantum Hall plateau> occupies a finite field interval, and its onset is not an exact statement that $n_s=eB/h$ at that edge. The plot identifies $\nu=1$ from about 10 T onward but does not specify a unique plateau-centre field or the gate setting at which the trace was recorded. The boxed formula gives the result for any selected target field. For comparison, the nominal exact-filling fields are 12.39 T at the original density and 18.34 T at the unchanged positive 0.2 V density. Thus the graph must not be combined silently with the latter density as if its $\nu=1$ onset uniquely fixed the same gate bias.

